<title>AUSTRALIA: Australia sugar unaffected by Iraq suspension.</title>
<headline>Australia sugar unaffected by Iraq suspension.</headline>
<dateline>SYDNEY 1996-09-04</dateline>
<text>
<p>Suspension of the proposed United Nations' oil-for-food deal with Iraq would not affect Australian sugar exports, David Rutledge, chief executive of the Queensland Sugar Corp (QSC), told Reuters.</p>
<p>QSC had not done struck deals with Iraq as a result of the originally-proposed UN programme, he said.</p>
<p>&quot;As a result there's no consequence for us in its cacellation,&quot; he said.</p>
<p>There were no arrangements in the pipeline which would be affected, he said.  </p>
<p>At the end of May, Rutledge said QSC was &quot;having a look&quot; at Iraq as a potential market through the proposed oil-for-food arrangements. Australia had not sold sugar to Iraq in the past.</p>
<p>The oil-for-food deal, suspended on Tuesday after the U.S. missile attack on Iraq, would have allowed Iraq to sell US$2 billion worth of oil every six months to buy food and medicine.</p>
<p>QSC owns almost all of Australia's annual sugar crop.</p>
<p>-- Sydney Newsroom 61-2 9373-1800</p>
</text>
